    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "Merch 29, 1927\nMr'. Ches. A. Inaberen, Inc.,\nc/o Fyen Airlines,\nSan\nDiego, California.\nDear Mr. Iindbergh:\nI have just received your letter of the 26th with further\nreference to the rebuilt Wright Whirlwind.\nSeotty mas \"all lit up\" about the prospecte of getting a\nwielwind but I think from your description that he has been saved\nYou are quite right when you say that the performance\nof this motor is a remaricable tribute to the fright Company, and\nat least gives a good idea of the service that one can expect\nfrom a real Thirlwind.\nLast night I spent some time with Manford Mellider, who\ncame through St. Louis with Capt. G. R. Irwin.\nI was talling to\nErvin about new developments in comerelal seaplanes, and he told\nme that there were a number of types being manufactured on the\nmest cost.\nI have an idea, which\nmay some day materialize, wherein\nwe will need a flykig boat for use on the Mississippi. Preferably,\nit should have a metallle mull, deaigned so ae to resist ordinary\ndriftwood. \"lthout going to any special trouble, 1f you could get\nsome information along this 11ne for me, I would be very grateful.\nThe ship I may want will be a job for the water somewhat similar\nto my Travel-Air, but I do not believe the Travel-Air would be made\nentirely practical for river worle by the addition of pontoons.\nAm delighted to mow that your monoplane is progressing\nrapidly and that you plan to be ready to take off on the date\nnamed. That date, of course, we must contine to leep secret.\nwith lindest regards,\nand again thanking you for all\nthat you have done in my behalf, I am\nyury sincerely,\nTIMB: DH"
    ],
